INT. DISTRICT COURTHOUSE - CORRIDOR OUTSIDE COURTROOM 4B - DAY

Bench seating. A water cooler. A television mounted high plays the news without sound.

A WOMAN in a black suit paces with a folder. Her badge reads P. A. DONNELLY-ADEYEMI, Assistant District Attorney.

A MAN in a rumpled blazer stands near the cooler, tapping his phone. The placard on his briefcase reads "HAROLD OSEI-BRANDT, ESQ."

DONNELLY
Harold.

OSEI-BRANDT
Priscilla.

DONNELLY
You filed the motion at five a.m.

OSEI-BRANDT
Insomnia.

DONNELLY
It had a cover letter.

OSEI-BRANDT
I take pride in my cover letters.

She stops pacing. Stares at the TV. The ticker reads "BREAKING: LOCAL JUDGE RECUSES."

DONNELLY
(to herself)
Of course.

OSEI-BRANDT
Something?

DONNELLY
Nothing. Talking to the weather.

He chuckles. She does not.

DONNELLY (CONT'D)
(louder, to the whole corridor)
Anyone with a four o'clock in 4B should know it's been moved.

Nobody answers. A janitor wheels a mop bucket past, whistling.

OSEI-BRANDT
Wasted breath.

DONNELLY
Habit.

OSEI-BRANDT
Moved to where?

DONNELLY
Courtroom 7. New judge.

OSEI-BRANDT
Who?

DONNELLY
Judge Kowalczyk.

OSEI-BRANDT
(sinking onto the bench)
No.

DONNELLY
Yes.

OSEI-BRANDT
He hates me.

DONNELLY
He hates everyone. That's the beauty.

OSEI-BRANDT
Priscilla, I need a continuance.

DONNELLY
Harold, I need a miracle.

She sits beside him. Their briefcases touch. The TV flickers to a weather map: a hurricane swirl.

OSEI-BRANDT
(quietly)
Priscilla, if I say please.

DONNELLY
Don't.

OSEI-BRANDT
Please.

DONNELLY
(sighing)
Fine. A week.

He stands, extends a hand. She shakes it. Then she looks at the placard on his case, and at her own badge, and the cue in her notes.

DONNELLY (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Priscilla Anne. Not Priscilla.

OSEI-BRANDT
What?

DONNELLY
Nothing, Harold. Go.

He goes. She remains on the bench, watching the hurricane turn.
